summary.value:
The paper introduces RoboCerebra, a novel benchmark designed to evaluate System 2 reasoning capabilities—deliberative, high-level cognitive functions—in robotic manipulation tasks. The benchmark emphasizes long-horizon, compositional tasks that go beyond the short, reactive behaviors typically assessed in existing robotic datasets. RoboCerebra consists of:
1: A large-scale simulation dataset with 1,000 human-annotated trajectories across 100 task variants;
2: A hierarchical planning and execution framework combining a high-level vision-language model (VLM) planner and a low-level VLA controller;
3: A multi-dimensional evaluation protocol focusing on planning, reflection, and memory.
The authors benchmark several state-of-the-art VLMs (e.g., GPT-4o, Qwen2.5-VL) as System 2 modules and demonstrate improved performance under the proposed hierarchical framework.

